---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import CardList from "../components/CardList.astro";
import GlossaryTerm from "../components/GlossaryTerm.astro";
import heroParents from "../assets/images/hero-parents.avif";
---

<PageLayout
  title="For parents"
  description="Guidance and activities for parents navigating AI with their children and building understanding together through hands-on learning."
>
  <h1>For parents</h1>

  <Image src={heroParents} alt="Hero image: For parents" />

  <p>
    Your kids are using <GlossaryTerm id="chatgpt"
      >ChatGPT or Claude</GlossaryTerm
    >. Maybe for homework help, maybe for creative writing, maybe just to mess
    around. You&rsquo;ve probably used it yourself. But do you actually
    understand what&rsquo;s happening when you type a prompt and get a response?
  </p>

  <p>
    <em>LLMs Unplugged</em> gives you and your family a way to build that understanding
    together through hands-on activities. No mystification, no fear-mongering, just
    practical knowledge about how these tools actually work.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info">
    <p>
      These resources are under active development. If there&rsquo;s something
      you&rsquo;d like to see, please
      <a href="/about#get-in-touch">get in touch</a>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Family activity: understanding LLMs together</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>time</strong>: 60&ndash;90mins</li>
    <li><strong>for ages</strong>: 12+</li>
    <li>
      <strong>what you need</strong>: pen, paper, a six-sided die (or spinner),
      a children&rsquo;s book
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Here&rsquo;s a family-friendly version of the core workshop. Start with a
    brief introduction, then work through training your own model, using it to
    generate text, and finally trying a larger pre-trained model. The flow moves
    from &ldquo;how do patterns get into a model?&rdquo; to &ldquo;what comes
    out?&rdquo; to &ldquo;what happens at scale?&rdquo;
  </p>

  <CardList
    lessons={["intro", "training", "generation", "pretrained-generation"]}
  />

  <h3>Suggested timing</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>00:00 start the conversation</strong> &mdash; who in the family has
      used ChatGPT, Claude, or similar tools? what for? how do you think it works?
      where do the words come from? today we&rsquo;re going to build our own version&mdash;small
      and simple, but using the exact same approach as these <GlossaryTerm
        id="language-model">language models</GlossaryTerm
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>00:10 <a href="/lessons/training">Training</a></strong> &mdash; work
      together to count word patterns in a short text (the lesson provides a simple
      example, or choose a favourite children&rsquo;s book). This is the &ldquo;training&rdquo;
      step&mdash;where AI systems learn patterns from text.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>00:25 <a href="/lessons/generation">Generation</a></strong>
      &mdash; use your trained model and a die to generate new sentences. Take turns
      rolling and choosing words&mdash;make it playful. Read out your generated text
      and laugh at the weird results together.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong
        >00:45
        <a href="/lessons/pretrained-generation">Pre-trained model generation</a
        ></strong
      > &mdash; try using a bigger, pre-trained model (provided in the lesson). Notice
      how more training data leads to more coherent (and sometimes eerily realistic)
      output.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>00:55 close the loop</strong> &mdash; what surprised you about how this
      works? does this change how you think about ChatGPT and Claude? what questions
      do you still have?
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why this matters for families</h2>

  <p>
    Understanding how LLMs work helps you have better conversations with your
    kids about:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>homework and learning</strong>: when is it okay to use an LLM?
      what are the limitations? how do you check its work?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>privacy and data</strong>: these models learn from text&mdash;what
      does that mean for what you share with them?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>critical thinking</strong>: if you understand the pattern-matching
      underneath, you can better evaluate when an AI-generated answer might be
      plausible-sounding nonsense
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>career and future</strong>: AI is reshaping work and
      education&mdash;helping your kids understand these tools (rather than fear
      or worship them) is genuinely useful
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Learning alongside your kids</h2>

  <p>
    You don&rsquo;t need to be an expert to facilitate these activities. The
    lessons are designed to be self-explanatory, and the
    <a href="/lessons/training#instructor-notes">instructor notes</a> (yes, even for
    parents!) help you connect the hands-on work to modern AI systems.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some of the most valuable moments come from exploring together&mdash;when
    you&rsquo;re both surprised by how simple the core mechanism is, or when you
    both notice something strange about the generated text. These
    &ldquo;aha&rdquo; moments build shared understanding that makes future
    conversations about AI much more productive.
  </p>

  <h2>Notes</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      the activity above is streamlined for home use&mdash;if you have more time
      or particularly engaged kids, you can add the
      <a href="/lessons/weighted-randomness">Weighted randomness</a> lesson at the
      start (add 20mins) or the
      <a href="/lessons/sampling">Sampling</a> lesson at the end (add 15mins)
    </li>
    <li>
      younger kids (under 12) can participate with guidance, especially in the
      generation step&mdash;they often love the randomness and silliness of
      rolling dice to make sentences
    </li>
    <li>
      if you&rsquo;re doing this with multiple kids, have them each generate
      their own sentences and share them&mdash;the variety of outputs from the
      same model is part of the learning
    </li>
  </ul>
</PageLayout>
